From 716892498584022ba41c3bf318212a7eba9f60a1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Huy3ko <224625040+Huy3ko@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 00:44:43 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] fix(conflict): require a shared subject and a shared frame
 before a substitution counts

A substitution-category hit alone was treated as a contradiction. On a
production store all 45 open `preference` conflicts were false positives of
that rule: two documents that each merely name a value from the same exclusive
category (a model name in one, a provider in another) were reported as one
claim with two values.

Two gates now have to pass. The neighbourhood of each substituted token must
share MIN_SHARED_CONTEXT content words, and the two sentences must agree on
their FRAME -- the content words that remain once the substituted values are
removed -- with a Jaccard overlap of at least 0.7. Code fences and inline spans
are stripped first: `--data` is not a claim about a data layer.

The frame check replaces an exact "the two tokens after the value must match"
comparison. That comparison was positional and silently dropped real
contradictions in two shapes: a reordered clause ("the index runs on
postgresql" / "on mysql runs the index") and a value at the end of the sentence,
where nothing follows. Both score 1.0 on frame overlap; the intended hard
negative ("postgresql as the index" / "mysql for the tests") shares subject and
verb but differs in the object and scores 0.44. 0.7 sits between them.

Measured: 40 tests in cognition::triggers (including a table-driven matrix that
asserts zero false positives and zero false negatives), full library suite
2,286 passed / 0 failed against main = bee011a.
